  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Blitz all the spice paste ingredients to a smooth puree, adding a splash of the coconut milk if the blender needs help.",
      "minutes": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "Toast the desiccated coconut in a dry frying pan over a medium heat, stirring constantly, until it is a deep nutty brown. Pound or blitz it to a coarse paste. This is the kerisik and it is what gives rendang its body.",
      "minutes": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "Fry the spice paste in the casserole over a medium heat until it darkens and stops smelling raw, around eight minutes.",
      "minutes": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "Add the beef and turn it in the paste until every piece is coated.",
      "minutes": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Pour in both tins of coconut, add the lime leaves, cinnamon, tamarind, palm sugar and salt. Bring to a bare simmer.",
      "minutes": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Cook uncovered at the gentlest simmer for two to two and a half hours, stirring every twenty minutes or so. The liquid should reduce steadily and the oil will eventually separate out.",
      "minutes": 150
    },
    {
      "text": "Stir in the kerisik and keep cooking until the sauce is dark, thick and clinging to the meat rather than pooling around it. Taste for salt and tamarind.",
      "minutes": 20
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Better on the second day, and it freezes well for three months.",
    "If it looks like drying out before the beef is tender, add a splash of water rather than more coconut.",
    "Sample recipe that came with the site. Delete it once your own recipes are in."
